-- Reference price seed — new observations captured 2026-08-13T12:00:00Z.
-- Appends to public.price_quotes (migration 0019). APPEND ONLY: these are
-- NEW rows beside migration 0019's 2026-08-12 seed, not replacements —
-- captured_at + source are part of the natural key, so a same-priced SKU
-- re-observed a day later lands as a second row, which is the point of an
-- append-only series (see 0019 header, decision 4).
--
-- Source A: RunPod REST v2 catalogue (list-gpu-types + list-cpu-types),
-- called live 2026-08-13. Rows are filtered strictly by the API's own
-- communityCloud/secureCloud offered flags (decision 2 in 0019): a tier
-- whose flag is false is OMITTED even when the API still returns a nonzero
-- placeholder price for it, and a missing quote is a missing row, never a
-- zero. RunPod CPU flavours cpu3m/cpu5m (Memory-Optimized) have NO
-- serverless row for the same reason: serverlessPerVcpu=0 there is the
-- API's "not populated" sentinel for that tier, not a real $0 price.
--
-- Two edge cases the flag-only rule surfaces (flagged, not silently
-- resolved): B200 and B300 both carry communityCloud=true with a nonzero
-- community price yet maxCount.community=0 -- included per the flag (the
-- documented criterion), with max_cards:0 left visible in attrs so a reader
-- can see it is not currently provisionable on that tier. NVIDIA A40 shows
-- the opposite drift versus migration 0019's 2026-08-12 seed: it recorded
-- A40 community at 0.35, but the API now returns communityCloud:false for
-- A40 (secure remains 0.44) -- a real day-over-day change, so today's row
-- is secure-only, which is exactly what an append-only series is for.
--
-- Source B: web-researched vendor price bands (vendorExamples), one row per
-- named observation. Only classes whose native quote maps onto this
-- schema's CLOSED `unit` list without inventing a conversion are included:
-- GPU classes -> gpu-hour (direct). TPU classes -> gpu-hour too (there is no
-- tpu-hour/chip-hour unit in the schema; a TPU chip-hour is priced exactly
-- like a GPU-hour -- one accelerator, one hour -- so gpu-hour is the honest
-- nearest fit and attrs.unit_note records the substitution on every such
-- row). High-core-count CPU-instance classes (32/64/128 vCPU) and Apple
-- Silicon classes from input B are DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED: every example
-- there prices a whole multi-core instance (e.g. a 32-vCPU box at
-- $1.36/hr), and this schema's only CPU unit is vcpu-hour -- dividing the
-- instance price by its vCPU count to fit that unit would be exactly the
-- invented-conversion pattern 0019 itself refuses elsewhere (GiB vs GB, CU
-- vs USD, gb-month). RunPod's own CPU flavours (cpu3c/g/m, cpu5c/g/m) ARE
-- included because RunPod quotes those natively per-vCPU
-- (securePerVcpu/serverlessPerVcpu) -- no conversion needed.
--
-- `region` is 'global' throughout: none of these observations carry a region
-- dimension (RunPod quotes one price per SKU per cloud; the web-band
-- research did not capture per-region pricing either).

